T412	Entity 72584 72604	the romantic outcast
T413	Quotation 72606 72627	"Hello, Huckleberry!"
T414	Quotation 72628 72670	"Hello yourself, and see how you like it."
T415	Quotation 72671 72693	"What's that you got?"
T416	Quotation 72694 72705	"Dead cat."
T417	Quotation 72706 72772	"Lemme see him, Huck. My, he's pretty stiff. Where'd you get him?"
T418	Quotation 72773 72798	"Bought him off'n a boy."
T419	Quotation 72799 72819	"What did you give?"
T420	Quotation 72820 72891	"I give a blue ticket and a bladder that I got at the slaughter-house."
T421	Quotation 72892 72926	"Where'd you get the blue ticket?"
T422	Quotation 72927 72987	"Bought it off'n Ben Rogers two weeks ago for a hoop-stick."
T423	Quotation 72988 73029	"Say-- what is dead cats good for, Huck?"
T424	Quotation 73030 73058	"Good for? Cure warts with."
T425	Quotation 73059 73108	"No! Is that so? I know something that's better."
T426	Quotation 73109 73139	"I bet you don't. What is it?"
T427	Quotation 73140 73159	"Why, spunk-water."
T428	Quotation 73160 73214	"Spunk-water! I wouldn't give a dern for spunk-water."
T429	Quotation 73215 73263	"You wouldn't, wouldn't you? D'you ever try it?"
T430	Quotation 73264 73299	"No, I hain't. But Bob Tanner did."
T431	Quotation 73300 73318	"Who told you so!"
T432	Quotation 73319 73487	"Why, he told Jeff Thatcher, and Jeff told Johnny Baker, and Johnny told Jim Hollis, and Jim told Ben Rogers, and Ben told a nigger, and the nigger told me. There now!"
T433	Quotation 73488 73670	"Well, what of it? They'll all lie. Leastways all but the nigger. I don't know HIM. But I never see a nigger that WOULDN'T lie. Shucks! Now you tell me how Bob Tanner done it, Huck."
T434	Quotation 73671 73749	"Why, he took and dipped his hand in a rotten stump where the rain-water was."
T435	Quotation 73750 73767	"In the daytime?"
T436	Quotation 73768 73780	"Certainly."
T437	Quotation 73781 73810	"With his face to the stump?"
T438	Quotation 73811 73836	"Yes. Least I reckon so."
T439	Quotation 73837 73859	"Did he say anything?"
T440	Quotation 73860 73898	"I don't reckon he did. I don't know."
T441	Quotation 73899 74487	"Aha! Talk about trying to cure warts with spunk-water such a blame fool way as that! Why, that ain't a-going to do any good. You got to go all by yourself, to the middle of the woods, where you know there's a spunk-water stump, and just as it's midnight you back up against the stump and jam your hand in and say: 'Barley-corn, barley-corn, injun-meal shorts, Spunk-water, spunk-water, swaller these warts,' and then walk away quick, eleven steps, with your eyes shut, and then turn around three times and walk home without speaking to anybody.  Because if you speak the charm's busted."
T442	Quotation 74488 74564	"Well, that sounds like a good way; but that ain't the way Bob Tanner done."
T443	Quotation 74565 74890	"No, sir, you can bet he didn't, becuz he's the wartiest boy in this town; and he wouldn't have a wart on him if he'd knowed how to work spunk-water. I've took off thousands of warts off of my hands that way, Huck. I play with frogs so much that I've always got considerable many warts. Sometimes I take 'em off with a bean."
T444	Quotation 74891 74926	"Yes, bean's good. I've done that."
T445	Quotation 74927 74955	"Have you? What's your way?"
T446	Quotation 74956 75410	"You take and split the bean, and cut the wart so as to get some blood, and then you put the blood on one piece of the bean and take and dig a hole and bury it 'bout midnight at the crossroads in the dark of the moon, and then you burn up the rest of the bean. You see that piece that's got the blood on it will keep drawing and drawing, trying to fetch the other piece to it, and so that helps the blood to draw the wart, and pretty soon off she comes."
T447	Quotation 75411 75684	"Yes, that's it, Huck-- that's it; though when you're burying it if you say 'Down bean; off wart; come no more to bother me!' it's better. That's the way Joe Harper does, and he's been nearly to Coonville and most everywheres. But say-- how do you cure 'em with dead cats?"
T448	Quotation 75685 76145	"Why, you take your cat and go and get in the graveyard 'long about midnight when somebody that was wicked has been buried; and when it's midnight a devil will come, or maybe two or three, but you can't see 'em, you can only hear something like the wind, or maybe hear 'em talk; and when they're taking that feller away, you heave your cat after 'em and say, 'Devil follow corpse, cat follow devil, warts follow cat, I'm done with ye!' That'll fetch ANY wart."
T449	Quotation 76146 76186	"Sounds right. D'you ever try it, Huck?"
T450	Quotation 76187 76224	"No, but old Mother Hopkins told me."
T451	Quotation 76225 76286	"Well, I reckon it's so, then. Becuz they say she's a witch."
T452	Quotation 76287 76575	"Say! Why, Tom, I KNOW she is. She witched pap. Pap says so his own self. He come along one day, and he see she was a-witching him, so he took up a rock, and if she hadn't dodged, he'd a got her. Well, that very night he rolled off'n a shed wher' he was a layin drunk, and broke his arm."
T453	Quotation 76576 76636	"Why, that's awful. How did he know she was a-witching him?"
T454	Quotation 76637 76833	"Lord, pap can tell, easy. Pap says when they keep looking at you right stiddy, they're a-witching you. Specially if they mumble. Becuz when they mumble they're saying the Lord's Prayer backards."
T455	Quotation 76834 76878	"Say, Hucky, when you going to try the cat?"
T456	Quotation 76879 76946	"To-night. I reckon they'll come after old Hoss Williams to-night."
T457	Quotation 76947 77014	"But they buried him Saturday. Didn't they get him Saturday night?"
T458	Quotation 77015 77162	"Why, how you talk! How could their charms work till midnight?-- and THEN it's Sunday. Devils don't slosh around much of a Sunday, I don't reckon."
T459	Quotation 77163 77219	"I never thought of that. That's so. Lemme go with you?"
T460	Quotation 77220 77254	"Of course-- if you ain't afeard."
T461	Quotation 77255 77295	"Afeard! 'Tain't likely. Will you meow?"
T462	Quotation 77296 77515	"Yes-- and you meow back, if you get a chance. Last time, you kep' me a-meowing around till old Hays went to throwing rocks at me and says 'Dern that cat!' and so I hove a brick through his window-- but don't you tell."
T463	Quotation 77516 77628	"I won't. I couldn't meow that night, becuz auntie was watching me, but I'll meow this time. Say-- what's that?"
T464	Quotation 77629 77650	"Nothing but a tick."
T465	Quotation 77651 77673	"Where'd you get him?"
T466	Quotation 77674 77693	"Out in the woods."
T467	Quotation 77694 77721	"What'll you take for him?"
T468	Quotation 77722 77763	"I don't know. I don't want to sell him."
T469	Quotation 77764 77810	"All right. It's a mighty small tick, anyway."
T470	Quotation 77811 77926	"Oh, anybody can run a tick down that don't belong to them. I'm satisfied with it. It's a good enough tick for me."
T471	Quotation 77927 78004	"Sho, there's ticks a plenty. I could have a thousand of 'em if I wanted to."
T472	Quotation 78005 78144	"Well, why don't you? Becuz you know mighty well you can't. This is a pretty early tick, I reckon. It's the first one I've seen this year."
T473	Quotation 78145 78190	"Say, Huck-- I'll give you my tooth for him."
T474	Quotation 78191 78205	"Less see it."
